13.6 What remains open
Three topics of the index have no support in the repository yet, and stay proposals, not results:

routing and passive quantum switching in multi-carrier metropolitan topologies;
a dual-rail repeater chain with the filter on simulated devices, which would test this section’s multiplication rule against real gate noise;
gate teleportation and its reading in qg correlators.

24.4 Channels as affine maps of the readouts
A qubit is \(\rho=(I+r\cdot\sigma)/2\) with \(r=(qg_X,qg_Y,qg_Z)\). Every channel with Kraus operators acts as \(r'=Mr+c\) (notes §162, qang.channels.bloch_affine): the readouts after any noise are affine in those before it. The script checks it on 800 random states to \(2.8\cdot10^{-16}\). On the Chapter 1 scale:

amplitude damping \(\gamma\): \(m_Z\to(1-\gamma)m_Z+1000\gamma\), and \(m_X,m_Y\) are multiplied by \(\sqrt{1-\gamma}\) (with \(\gamma=0.3\): 0.7 and 0.837);
dephasing: leaves \(m_Z\) alone; depolarizing: multiplies the three readouts by the same number and keeps the qang angle;
T1/T2 relaxation for a time \(t\): \(m_Z\to e^{-t/T_1}m_Z+(1-e^{-t/T_1})(1-2p_1)\cdot1000\) and \(m_{X,Y}\to e^{-t/T_2}m_{X,Y}\) (with \(t=0.5\), \(T_1=1\), \(T_2=0.8\): 0.6065 and 0.5353, which are \(e^{-0.5}\) and \(e^{-0.625}\)).

It is the Chapter 1 picture carried over to noise: every channel moves the readout point inside the sphere by a linear rule.

25.6 Overlaps: inversion test against swap test
The fidelity \(F=|\langle x|y\rangle|^2\) of two unary states can be estimated two ways (§149). The inversion test loads \(x\), undoes the loader of \(y\) and reads the probability of one basis state: 5 qubits, everything conserves the weight. The swap test uses 11 qubits and an ancilla, and reads \(P(\text{ancilla}=0)=(1+F)/2\). The script checks both formulas with exact states (\(3\cdot10^{-16}\) and \(6\cdot10^{-16}\)). On six IBM noise models:

with the filter, the inversion test cuts its error 3.8 to 11.2 times, down to 0.010–0.036;
in the swap test the answer is on the ancilla, outside the symmetry, and the filter gains only 1.4–1.8 times (error 0.089–0.239);
a fidelity classifier (40 flowers) keeps its noiseless accuracy with both methods, because comparing two fidelities cancels the common shrink;
but the test “are they equal?” needs the absolute value: for identical states the filtered inversion test reads 0.92–1.00 and the raw swap test 0.20–0.65.







